<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian Democracy (Italy)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1943–1994</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Democrazia Cristiana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian Democracy was a Catholic, centrist political party that dominated Italian politics from 1946 to 1994, leading or taking part in every government. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi">Alcide De Gasperi</a> it anchored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and European integration. It collapsed in the early 1990s amid the Tangentopoli corruption scandals and split into several parties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-democracy-italy.jpg" alt="Christian Democracy (Italy)" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Democrazia Cristiana Vector: C records · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DC_Party_Logo_(1968-1992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
